1161:== 1. SIBLING PARITY
1162:N/A: the repair adds rows, not arms.  The five arms and their parity were PRETABLE.out section 1.
1163:
1164:== 2. CLAIM -> ROW (every ADDED or CHANGED sentence asserting an observable consequence)
1165:* dev_sgi_re.c, F1: "On a START-tagged write the register store has already landed in the caller and stays" -- no row
1166:  reads an MTE register after a START; disclosed (probe contract NOT CLAIMED, and PRETABLE.out section 2).
1167:  "a START-tagged READ reaches here too (filed separately)" -- no row drives a START-tagged read; disclosed in the
1168:  contract and filed (mtestartread).  "either way the caller's common tail still answers" -- `after status', the
1169:  `*surv' rows (a write); the read tail has no row (same disclosure).
1170:* probe contract: "S1 and S2 at EVERY destination label whose arm writes guest memory ... each in BOTH latch states"
1171:  -> the 20 cell rows of section 3; "a new site's bit ALIASED onto a pre-existing one" -> `xbit primed' + `xbit
1172:  texts'; "the paint is read ONCE, at its end" (the sweep) -> `mask sweep' (unchanged); the NOT CLAIMED additions
1173:  (source 1, the printed VALUES, an MTE register written by a decline, a START-tagged READ) are explicit no-row
1174:  sentences.
1175:
1176:== 3. GUARD FIELD vs BODY FIELD -- the skipped-dispatch cells (2026-09-25 clause, first reuse; fit checked)
1177:S1's and S2's `return' bypasses `switch (dst_tlb)'.  Guest-observable labels: A, B, C (tiled arm writes via
1178:horrible_getputpixel) and LIN_A, LIN_B (memory_rw fill); 3 (TEX), 6 (CLIP), 7 reach `default:', which only prints
1179:(dev_sgi_re.c:1732-1733) -- excluded as host-debug only.  Each repair path tests its OWN latch bit, so the floor is
1180:labels x {CLEAR, SET} per site.  The partition the body induces on each lvalue: d->warned -- one bit per site, tested
1181:as a case (set / clear); dst_tlb -- not read by S1/S2's body, an axis only through the bypassed dispatch.
1182:    cell                 row(s) that hold it
1183:    S1 A   CLEAR         stip a                       S1 A   SET   stip copy; latch paint (s1again)
1184:    S1 B   CLEAR         stip b clr          (new)    S1 B   SET   stip b
1185:    S1 C   CLEAR         stip c clr          (new)    S1 C   SET   stip c
1186:    S1 LA  CLEAR         stip la clr         (new)    S1 LA  SET   stip lin a
1187:    S1 LB  CLEAR         stip lb clr         (new)    S1 LB  SET   stip lin b
1188:    S2 A   CLEAR         src 3; latch paint           S2 A   SET   src 4..7, src 3 copy
1189:    S2 B   CLEAR         src b clr           (new)    S2 B   SET   src 3 b       (new)
1190:    S2 C   CLEAR         src c clr           (new)    S2 C   SET   src 3 c       (new)
1191:    S2 LA  CLEAR         src la clr          (new)    S2 LA  SET   src 3 lin
1192:    S2 LB  CLEAR         src lb clr          (new)    S2 LB  SET   src 3 lin b   (new)
1193:The ACCEPT face at each label (guard false): A (tile mask, after fill, src c fill), LIN_A / LIN_B (lin fill a / b) and,
1194:new, B and C (fill b ok, fill c ok).  The latch-conditioned accept-face variants are DISCLOSED (clause e), not rowed.
1195:
1196:== 4. CHANNEL -- per new row, the mutant it is for and the observable that differs under it
1197:    stip/src <lab> clr   the CLEAR-conditioned label exemption at <lab>    the paint at that label is overwritten
1198:    src 3 b / 3 c / 3 lin b   the SET-conditioned exemption at B / C / LB      the paint is overwritten
1199:    fill b ok / fill c ok     S1 or S2 grown to refuse every transfer to B / C  the legal fill does NOT land (paint kept)
1200:    xbit primed          (setup proof) a priming access that stops announcing  fewer than 13 priming texts
1201:    xbit texts           a new site's bit aliased onto a pre-existing one      that site's count reads 0
1202:Every new linear cell runs at depth 8, BYTEMASK 0xffffffff, COPY clear, so S3-S5 cannot hide an escape; every tiled
1203:cell maps its own tile (B and C are mapped where they are first used) and repaints before its transfer -- A, B and C
1204:share one physical page, so an unrepainted cell could red on its neighbour's write.  CHECKED AT WRITING TIME: no new
1205:row's channel is a survival oracle at a refusal-effect site.
1206:
1207:== 5. CLAUSE (c) -- the null medium
1208:`null' (/bin/true) in this table: every session dies, every row that reads a value or a survival reds.  Measured, not
1209:claimed, below.
1210:
1211:== 6. THE (d) DEPENDS-ON ENUMERATION for the repair, and what table 3 measures
1212:* the 20 latch-conditioned label exemptions (S1, S2) x {A, B, C, LA, LB} x {SET, CLEAR} -- each predicted to red ONLY
1213:  its own cell's row(s), section 3.  A plain label exemption exempts a superset of cells; its detection follows from
1214:  either conditioned variant's and is recorded as that consequence, not as a measurement.
1215:* one alias per new latch bit onto a pre-existing bit no other session sets: STIPPLE -> 10, SRC -> 6, COPY -> 11,
1216:  DEPTH -> 12, BYTEMASK -> 8 -- each predicted to red `xbit texts' only.  Every other (site, pre-existing bit) pair
1217:  -- 60 of them -- is covered BY CONSTRUCTION via `xbit primed' (all 13 set) AND `xbit texts' (each site announced
1218:  once): every writer of d->warned is `|=', so a site whose bit equals a primed one takes its silent branch.  Not
1219:  covered: a compound mutation that also CLEARS warning state.
1220:* the ACCEPT face at B and C: S1 and S2 grown by `|| dst_tlb == B' / `== C' -- each predicted to red `fill b ok' /
1221:  `fill c ok' (and nothing else: the refused transfers stay refused).
1222:* `head' -- HEAD 2951694f's build, clause (a) for the repaired instrument: every row that drives a refused transfer
1223:  dies there; the new rows must all red, the controls stay green.
1224:TABLE 1 (34) and TABLE 2 (2) stand as OLD-INSTRUMENT evidence for the 53 rows they scored: the repair APPENDS -- the
1225:S2 session's new transfers come after its last existing check, the ACCEPT session's after its last, and the other new
1226:rows are new sessions -- so no existing row's command sequence before its read changed.  Aggregate rows CAN change
1227:value under a mutant (`src once' counts the whole session: a missing-suppression mutant now reads 10 where it read 7;
1228:a death in the appended tail reads `died') -- both still red.  This is a source-derived argument, not a re-measurement.
1229:```
